analysis:
Integration review of the GLM-5.3-Flash variable-frame-rate (VFR) video PR. Visible changes: changelog.d/glm-variable-frame-rate-video.md; docs/architecture/inference.md (head ~810-813, ~829); docs/provider/cli-reference.md (line 52); the libs/mlx-swift-lm submodule pointer (7bed8216 to 6aef0a5d); and the test-only GLM5NextMediaRoutingTests.swift, which adds CryptoKit, a stub tokenizer, SHA-256-pinned fixture loading and a scheduler-level readiness test.

No production source is visible. The actual processor change (GLM5NextProcessor frame indexing at i / nominalFrameRate) sits inside the unreviewed submodule delta, so I cannot confirm it. The docs say the unchanged bounds still apply: 2 FPS sampler, 512 MiB decoded-frame cap (maximumDecodedBytes), 28,532 visual-token prepared-pixel cap, and GLM5NextVisionBudget reservation. They also say media stays excluded from prefix lookup and capture (permitsHybridCheckpoint), so the T-041 posture and SEC-035 are unaffected.

No encryption, attestation (TB-011, T-053), key custody, coordinator wire or billing behavior changes in the supplied evidence. The test code reads repo fixtures only and writes nothing, so it adds no production attack surface and does not touch T-050.

Residual unverified contract, not a finding: the accepting change broadens untrusted-consumer media parsing (TB-007). The next pass should inspect the submodule diff for:
- a frame-count or duration bound before the full presentation-order index pass;
- safe handling of a zero, NaN or absurd nominalFrameRate;
- that the decoded-frame cap holds when sampling by synthetic timestamps;
- that no plaintext video is written to temp files (T-050);
- that the media reservation still covers VFR clips.
This is flagged for deeper review because the submodule contents were not supplied.
